Instruction: Read the following segment of a merger agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the “ability to consummate” concept subject to Material Adverse Effect (MAE) carveouts under this agreement?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Merger Agreement:
“Material Adverse Impact” means any change, event, or circumstance that (a) has had or would reasonably be expected to have a material adverse effect on the business, assets, liabilities, financial condition, or operating results of the Company and its Subsidiaries, or (b) would, in the aggregate, prevent, materially delay or materially impair the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions contemplated by this Agreement. Notwithstanding the foregoing, for purposes of clause (a) only, no change, event or circumstance (whether occurring individually or in combination) that arises as a result of any of the following shall be deemed to constitute a Material Adverse Impact: 
   (i) general economic downturns or changes in global market conditions that affect companies across the board;
   (ii) shifts in financial market conditions, including interest rate fluctuations and credit market variations;
   (iii) regulatory changes, including modifications in laws or accounting standards, that impact the industry generally;
   (iv) force majeure events such as natural disasters, pandemics, or other uncontrollable events, except where the impact is demonstrably disproportionate relative to other industry participants.
Furthermore, it is expressly provided that notwithstanding any carveouts applicable under clause (a), the events contemplated in clause (b) relating to the Company’s ability to consummate the transactions are not subject to the exclusions set forth above. 

